add(H2('5.2 Study 3: false-call reduction on production AOI data'));
const fx = (r, a, lo, hi) => `${p1(r[a])} (${p1(r[lo])}–${p1(r[hi])})`;
const rr = (k) => `${p1(rt3[k].mean)} (${p1(rt3[k].p2_5)}–${p1(rt3[k].p97_5)})`;
add(P(`Table 4 and Figure 3 summarize Study 3. The retrospective random-split evaluation promised an excellent screen: ROC-AUC ${f(rt3.auc.mean, 3)}, realized slip of ${p1(rt3.slip_thr.mean)} against the 1% target, and volume reduction of ${pct(rt3.vr_thr.mean)}. In the deployment replay with immediate labels, ROC-AUC was ${f(s3r(0).auc, 3)}, realized slip was ${fx(s3r(0), 'slip_thr', 'slip_thr_lo', 'slip_thr_hi')}, more than twice the target, and volume reduction fell to ${pct(s3r(0).vr_thr)}. Slip exceeded the target in ${s3r(0).weeks_slip_above_target} of ${s3r(0).weeks} weeks and reached ${p1(s3r(0).max_weekly_slip)} in the worst week. At a fixed 40% release rate, slip was ${fx(s3r(0), 'slip_fixed', 'slip_fixed_lo', 'slip_fixed_hi')} in the replay against ${rr('slip_fixed')} under random splitting; the intervals do not overlap. With ${s3r(0).defects.toLocaleString('en-US')} true defects in the replay period, this gap is estimated precisely; realized slip under the threshold rule is less precise because the threshold itself changes from week to week, and its interval is wide enough to include the random-split estimate, although the point estimate is more than twice as high.`));
add(P(`Unlike in Study 1, label delay made no detectable further difference. Realized slip ranged from ${p1(Math.min(...s3slips))} to ${p1(Math.max(...s3slips))} across delays of 0 to 14 days, every paired contrast with immediate labels had a 95% interval that included zero, and a model frozen at the start of deployment performed about as well as one retrained weekly (slip ${p1(s3f.slip_thr)}). Retraining on recent labels did not reduce the week-to-week variation in slip, which suggests that it reflects changes that past labels, however recent, do not anticipate. On this line the error that matters is the use of later production in a random split, not a delay of days in label availability.`));
add(tcap('Table 4. ', `Study 3: releasing AOI alarms without manual inspection. Threshold rule: threshold set for 1% slip on recent labelled history. Fixed rule: the 40% lowest-risk alarms are released (random release gives slip 40%). Replay: ${s3r(0).n.toLocaleString('en-US')} alarms, ${s3r(0).defects.toLocaleString('en-US')} true defects, ${s3r(0).weeks} weeks. Intervals: day-block bootstrap (replay) or 2.5–97.5% range over ${N.s3.meta.repeats} splits (random split).`));
