add(P(`Other models and threshold procedures did not remove the gap (Table 5). Across the three models, random-split evaluation estimated realized slip of ${p1(rng3('retro_slip_thr')[0])}–${p1(rng3('retro_slip_thr')[1])} for the 1% rule, whereas deployment realized ${p1(rng3('slip_thr')[0])}–${p1(rng3('slip_thr')[1])} and exceeded the target in ${Math.min(...MODS.map(v => s3m(v).weeks_above))}–${Math.max(...MODS.map(v => s3m(v).weeks_above))} of 11 weeks; at a fixed 40% release rate, deployed slip was ${p1(rng3('slip_fixed')[0])}–${p1(rng3('slip_fixed')[1])} against ${p1(rng3('retro_slip_fixed')[0])}–${p1(rng3('retro_slip_fixed')[1])} under random splitting. Changing the threshold procedure moved the operating point along a trade-off rather than closing the gap. Calibrating on the last week alone made slip worse (${p1(s3m('thr_recent_week').slip_thr)}), because a week contains too few true defects to set a 1% threshold. Halving the target gave slip of ${p1(s3m('thr_target_0.5').slip_thr)} with volume reduction of ${pct(s3m('thr_target_0.5').vr_thr)}. The confidence-bound rule met the requirement on average (slip ${p1(s3m('thr_ucb').slip_thr)}, 95% CI ${p1(s3m('thr_ucb').slip_thr_lo)}–${p1(s3m('thr_ucb').slip_thr_hi)}; target exceeded in ${s3m('thr_ucb').weeks_above} of 11 weeks) but released only ${pct(s3m('thr_ucb').vr_thr)} of false calls, against the ${pct(rt3.vr_thr.mean)} promised by random splitting. The confidence-bound rule reduced risk rather than guaranteeing compliance. Because it tests thresholds in increasing order and stops at the first failure, it is a fixed-sequence test that controls the probability of exceeding the target at the nominal level when calibration and future alarms are exchangeable (Angelopoulos et al. 2021); under drift it carries no guarantee: its bound is computed from past calibration data, does not account for drift, and the target was still exceeded in ${s3m('thr_ucb').weeks_above} of 11 weeks. Reducing slip towards the requirement under drift came at a large cost in benefit, which a random-split evaluation hides. The gradient-boosting results in Table 5 differ slightly from Table 4 because a different random subsample of false calls was used for fitting; both remain far from the random-split estimate.`));
