const rng_ = (arr, k, fn) => `${fn(Math.min(...arr.map(r => r[k])))}–${fn(Math.max(...arr.map(r => r[k])))}`;
const full = (m) => sel(m)[0];
add(P(`With a 7-day discovery delay and identical scores, the online threshold again dominated the static rule (slip ${p1(full('online (full feedback)').slip)}, volume reduction ${pct(full('online (full feedback)').volume_reduction_net)}, against ${p1(full('static 1% rule (full feedback)').slip)} and ${pct(full('static 1% rule (full feedback)').volume_reduction_net)}) and gave lower slip than the sliding-window (${p1(full('sliding-window quantile (28 d) (full feedback)').slip)}) and exponentially weighted (${p1(full('exponentially weighted quantile (14 d) (full feedback)').slip)}) quantile rules at similar volume reduction; the Clopper–Pearson window rule had the lowest slip (${p1(full('Clopper-Pearson window rule (28 d) (full feedback)').slip)}) but released only ${pct(full('Clopper-Pearson window rule (28 d) (full feedback)').volume_reduction_net)} of false calls (Supplementary Table S7). Under selective feedback, auditing was indispensable. Without audits the threshold could only rise and slip reached ${p1(selq(0)[0].slip)}. With 1% audits, realized slip spanned ${rng_(selq(0.01), 'slip', p1)} across audit randomizations, because a handful of audited defects drove the threshold. With 10% and 20% audits, slip was ${rng_([...selq(0.1), ...selq(0.2)], 'slip', p1)}, with ${rng_([...selq(0.1), ...selq(0.2)], 'audit_share_of_alarms', p1)} of alarms audited and net volume reduction of ${rng_([...selq(0.1), ...selq(0.2)], 'volume_reduction_net', pct)}; auditing every released alarm gave no savings at all. The bound of Proposition 3 held in every run (largest ratio of deviation to bound ${f(Math.max(...N.sel.filter(r => r.bound != null).map(r => r.dev / r.bound)), 2)}), but at this horizon it is wide (${rng_(N.sel.filter(r => r.q === 0.1 || r.q === 0.2), 'bound', p1)} for q = 0.1–0.2), so it supports long-run claims rather than weekly ones. The observable finite-horizon bound of Corollary 1 was valid but uninformative here: counting every recently released alarm as potentially defective gave bounds above ${pct(Math.min(...N.fh.map(r => r.pending_bound)))} at every week end, whereas the actual gap between slip over all defects and over confirmed defects never exceeded ${p1(Math.max(...N.fh.map(r => r.pending_gap)))}.`));
add(H2('5.3 When does label delay matter? Model ageing'));
add(P(`Figure 4 shows why label delay mattered on SECOM but not on the AOI line. SECOM models lost value quickly with age: top-10% recall of the random forest fell from ${f(ag('SECOM', 'random_forest', '0-7').value)} at 0–7 days to ${f(ag('SECOM', 'random_forest', '7-14').value)} at 7–14 days and ${f(ag('SECOM', 'random_forest', '28-42').value)} at 28–42 days, and that of logistic regression from ${f(ag('SECOM', 'logistic', '0-7').value)} to ${f(ag('SECOM', 'logistic', '7-14').value)} and ${f(ag('SECOM', 'logistic', '28-42').value)}, reaching the random-inspection rate of 0.10 within one to three weeks, although intervals are wide. The AOI model did not: slip at a 40% release rate was ${p1(ag('AOI', 'hgb', '0-7').value)} at 0–7 days and ${p1(Math.min(...aoiAges))}–${p1(Math.max(...aoiAges))} at older ages, with mean ROC-AUC of ${f(Math.min(...N.age.filter(r => r.dataset === 'AOI').map(r => r.mean_auc)), 2)}–${f(Math.max(...N.age.filter(r => r.dataset === 'AOI').map(r => r.mean_auc)), 2)} at every age. Measured as the share of a model's first-week advantage over random selection that remained one week later, SECOM retained ${pct(Math.max(0, keep('SECOM', 'logistic', 0.10, true)))} (logistic regression) and ${pct(Math.max(0, keep('SECOM', 'random_forest', 0.10, true)))} (random forest), while the AOI model retained ${pct(keep('AOI', 'hgb', 0.40, false))}. On the AOI line the loss happens in one step, between the random-split estimate (slip ${p1(rt3.slip_fixed.mean)}) and any future week, and does not grow with age. In these two settings, label delay cost value where the ageing curve fell over the span of the delay. We regard this as an empirically supported mechanism rather than a general law: ageing can also reflect changes in training-set size, defect prevalence and feature availability, and two datasets cannot establish how well an ageing curve predicts the loss from a given delay. Whether it does, across further datasets and controlled drift, is an open question; meanwhile an ageing curve is a cheap diagnostic of whether shortening label delay is worth investigating.`));
add(img('fig4_model_ageing.png', 600, 250));
add(caption('Figure 4. Model ageing. Performance of models fitted at weekly anchors as a function of the age of the model when applied, pooled over anchors, with 95% day-block bootstrap intervals. Left: SECOM, share of failures caught when the top 10% are flagged (dotted line: random inspection). Right: AOI, slip when the 40% lowest-risk alarms are released (dashed line: random-split estimate; random release gives 40%).'));
add(H2('5.4 Where does the gap come from?'));
const A40 = lk('AOI', 'hgb', 'act_batch'), A40c = lk('AOI', 'hgb', 'act_causal'), A1 = lk('AOI', 'hgb', 'act_slip1');
const RFb = lk('SECOM', 'random_forest', 'act_batch'), RFc = lk('SECOM', 'random_forest', 'act_causal'), LRb = lk('SECOM', 'logistic', 'act_batch'), LRc = lk('SECOM', 'logistic', 'act_causal');
const ci = (r, k, fn) => `${fn(r[k])} [${fn(r[k + '_lo'])}, ${fn(r[k + '_hi'])}]`;
const pp = (x) => minus((100 * x).toFixed(1));
add(P(`Table 7 separates the sources of the gap. On the AOI line the decisive factor was whether training could include units from the same week. With the 40% batch release, slip was ${p1(A40.W_share)} when the size-matched training sample could include other alarms from the evaluated week, ${p1(A40.M_share)} when the whole week was excluded and ${p1(A40.C_share)} for the chronological model; the difference W − M was ${ci(A40, 'WminusM_share', pp)} percentage points (95% intervals in brackets), whereas using later production made no detectable difference (M − C: ${ci(A40, 'MminusC_share', pp)} points). Alarms from the same week share boards, components and conditions, so a random split tests the model largely on close neighbours of its training data. On SECOM the decisive factor was later production: with batch flagging, the random forest's lift was ${f(RFb.M_lift)} when training could use later weeks and ${f(RFb.C_lift)} when it could not (difference ${ci(RFb, 'MminusC_lift', f)}), and with causal thresholds ${f(RFc.M_lift)} against ${f(RFc.C_lift)} (difference ${ci(RFc, 'MminusC_lift', f)}), whereas same-week units added little (W − M: ${ci(RFb, 'WminusM_lift', f)} with batch flagging). With only ${RFb.positives} failures in the evaluated half-windows, the logistic-regression contrasts were not statistically clear. The two sources agree with the ageing results: SECOM models benefit from later production, which indicates drift, whereas on the AOI line value is lost as soon as a model leaves the period it was trained in. Repeating the experiment with two further independent random half-weeks and training samples preserved the direction of every main contrast: on the AOI line W − M for the 40% batch release ranged from ${pp(Math.min(...sdz('AOI', 'hgb', 'act_batch').map(r => r.WminusM_share)))} to ${pp(Math.max(...sdz('AOI', 'hgb', 'act_batch').map(r => r.WminusM_share)))} points and was significant in all three samples, and on SECOM M − C for the random forest's lift ranged from ${f(Math.min(...sdz('SECOM', 'random_forest', 'act_batch').map(r => r.MminusC_lift)))} to ${f(Math.max(...sdz('SECOM', 'random_forest', 'act_batch').map(r => r.MminusC_lift)))} (batch flagging) and was significant in ${sdz('SECOM', 'random_forest', 'act_batch').filter(r => r.MminusC_lift_lo > 0).length} of three; with causal rules the AOI contrast was significant in one of three samples, so its size is less certain than its sign.`));
add(P(`Causal decisions led to the same conclusions as batch decisions. On the AOI line, the causal 40th-percentile threshold released ${pct(A40c.C_rate)} of alarms in the chronological design, with slip of ${p1(A40c.C_share)} against ${p1(A40.C_share)} for the batch release. On SECOM, causal thresholds inspected between ${pct(Math.min(RFc.C_rate, LRc.C_rate))} and ${pct(Math.max(RFc.C_rate, LRc.C_rate))} of units in the chronological design, and lift stayed close to that of random inspection (${f(LRc.C_lift)} and ${f(RFc.C_lift)}). The look-ahead implicit in batch decisions therefore did not drive the results above. Values in Table 7 differ slightly from Tables 3 and 4 because 20% of each training set was held out for calibration and only half of each week was evaluated.`));
add(tcap('Table 7. ', `Sources of the gap between random-split and deployment performance (immediate labels; same evaluated half-week units for all designs). C: trained on earlier units only. M: size-matched random sample from all other weeks, earlier or later. W: as M but may include units from the same week. AOI: slip (%), lower is better; random-split estimate for the 40% batch release ${p1(rt3.slip_fixed.mean)}. SECOM: lift (1 = random); random-split estimate ${f(retro('random_forest', 'recall_at_budget').mean / N.s1.budget)} (random forest) and ${f(retro('logistic', 'recall_at_budget').mean / N.s1.budget)} (logistic). Differences with 95% paired day-block bootstrap intervals in brackets.`));
add(table(['Dataset – decision', 'C', 'M', 'W', 'W − M (same week)', 'M − C (later production)'], [
  ['AOI – release lowest 40% (batch)', pp(A40.C_share), pp(A40.M_share), pp(A40.W_share), ci(A40, 'WminusM_share', pp), ci(A40, 'MminusC_share', pp)],
  ['AOI – release below 40th pct. (causal)', pp(A40c.C_share), pp(A40c.M_share), pp(A40c.W_share), ci(A40c, 'WminusM_share', pp), ci(A40c, 'MminusC_share', pp)],
  ['AOI – 1%-slip rule (causal)', pp(A1.C_share), pp(A1.M_share), pp(A1.W_share), ci(A1, 'WminusM_share', pp), ci(A1, 'MminusC_share', pp)],
  ['SECOM RF – top 10% (batch), lift', f(RFb.C_lift), f(RFb.M_lift), f(RFb.W_lift), ci(RFb, 'WminusM_lift', f), ci(RFb, 'MminusC_lift', f)],
  ['SECOM RF – 90th pct. (causal), lift', f(RFc.C_lift), f(RFc.M_lift), f(RFc.W_lift), ci(RFc, 'WminusM_lift', f), ci(RFc, 'MminusC_lift', f)],
  ['SECOM LR – top 10% (batch), lift', f(LRb.C_lift), f(LRb.M_lift), f(LRb.W_lift), ci(LRb, 'WminusM_lift', f), ci(LRb, 'MminusC_lift', f)],
  ['SECOM LR – 90th pct. (causal), lift', f(LRc.C_lift), f(LRc.M_lift), f(LRc.W_lift), ci(LRc, 'WminusM_lift', f), ci(LRc, 'MminusC_lift', f)],
], [2700, 700, 700, 700, 2100, 2100]));
add(H2('5.5 Study 2: how much immediate-result evaluation overstates value'));
const t4 = [];
['nominal', 'moderate_shift', 'severe_shift', 'high_defect'].forEach(sc => POLS.forEach(p => {
  const a = sm(sc, 0, p), b = sm(sc, 3, p), c = bi(sc, p, 3);
  t4.push([`${SCN[sc]} – ${p}`, f(a.mean_net, 1), f(b.mean_net, 1), `${f(c.bias, 1)} (${f(c.bias_lo, 1)}, ${f(c.bias_hi, 1)})`, f(a.mean_defects, 1), f(b.mean_defects, 1), f(a.mean_oos_periods, 2), f(b.mean_oos_periods, 2)]);
}));
add(P(`Supplementary Table S1 compares each policy evaluated with immediate results (L = 0) against the same policy with a three-period report delay, using the same seeds. In the nominal and severe-shift scenarios the immediate-result evaluation overstated mean net value by ${f(biasMin, 1)} to ${f(biasMax, 1)} units for every policy. In the nominal scenario this amounts to ${f(Math.min(...nomRel), 0)}–${f(Math.max(...nomRel), 0)}% of the true value. In the severe-shift scenario it exceeds the true value itself, because a delayed policy earns little: for example, AC was worth ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, 'AC').mean_net, 1)} units under immediate results but ${f(sm('severe_shift', 3, 'AC').mean_net, 1)} under a three-period delay. Overstatement grew roughly linearly with delay (Supplementary Figure S1).`));
add(P(`Figure 5 shows that two different mechanisms produce the overstatement. When the process is stable, almost all of it is lost production: every policy must wait for pilot results before deploying, so a delay of L periods removes about L production periods from the run. After a severe shift, the monitoring policies (WS, AC, AE) lose almost nothing in deployment time; their loss is extra defect cost, because the shift is detected L periods later. Each of them produced ${f(Math.min(...extraDefSevere), 1)}–${f(Math.max(...extraDefSevere), 1)} more defective units per run, and out-of-specification periods for AC rose from ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, 'AC').mean_oos_periods, 2)} to ${f(sm('severe_shift', 3, 'AC').mean_oos_periods, 2)}. FX, which in practice never rolls back, shows only the deployment-time component. The remedy therefore differs: faster pilot reporting shortens time to deployment, whereas faster in-process reporting limits exposure after a shift.`));
addS(tcap('Table S1. ', 'Mean net value, defective units and out-of-specification periods per run with immediate results (L = 0) and a three-period delay (L = 3). Overstatement = V(0) − V(3), with 95% bootstrap interval. Base costs (c_d = 35, R = 6); 1,000 paired seeds.'));
addS(table(['Scenario – policy', 'V(0)', 'V(3)', 'Overstatement', 'Defects L=0', 'Defects L=3', 'OOS L=0', 'OOS L=3'], t4, [2200, 800, 800, 1650, 900, 900, 850, 900]));
addS(img('fig5_sim_value_vs_delay.png', 600, 233));
addS(caption('Figure S1. Mean net value (left, centre) and out-of-specification production periods (right) as a function of report delay. 1,000 paired seeds per point; bootstrap intervals are narrower than the markers.'));
add(img('fig6_bias_decomposition.png', 560, 272));
add(caption('Figure 5. Decomposition of the immediate-result overstatement V(0) − V(3) into its cost components (base costs). Bars above zero increase the overstatement; bars below zero offset it.'));

add(H2('5.6 Study 2: does delay change the choice of policy?'));
const arlRows = [0.035, 0.06, 0.10, 0.12, 0.20, 0.24].map(p => [f(p, 3), f(arl('CUSUM', p).arl, 1), f(arl('EWMA', p).arl, 1), f(arl('CUSUM', p).p_signal_within_12, 3), f(arl('EWMA', p).p_signal_within_12, 3)]);
add(P(`Report delay changed absolute values far more than it changed rankings. Across the ${N.s2.n_cells} combinations of scenario, cost pair and delay, the policy that ranked first under immediate results also ranked first under the true delay in ${nAgree} cases (${pct(N.s2.agreement_rate)}). All disagreements but one occurred in the high-defect scenario, where every policy correctly abandons almost every run and the leading values differ by less than two units; the largest regret was ${f(N.s2.max_regret)} units, and outside the high-defect scenario it was at most ${f(N.s2.regret_outside_high_defect, 2)}. Because many cells are near-ties, the agreement rate itself is sensitive to simulation noise (Section 5.8); regret is the more reliable summary. In this design, an immediate-result evaluation would mislead mainly about the size of the business case, not about which policy to adopt.`));
add(P(`The cost ratio, by contrast, determined the ranking. In the severe-shift scenario at the base costs, FX had the highest net value (${f(rk('severe_shift', 0, 35, 6).net_FX, 1)} against ${f(rk('severe_shift', 0, 35, 6).net_AC, 1)} for AC), even though it never rolled back and spent ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, 'FX').mean_oos_periods, 1)} periods per run out of specification against ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, 'AC').mean_oos_periods, 1)} for AC. The reason is that with c_d = 35 the post-shift defect rate of 0.24 is close to break-even (0.23), so continuing to produce costs little in the value metric. With c_d = 70, AC ranked first at every rollback cost (Supplementary Table S3). An evaluation that reports only net value would therefore recommend the policy with ${f(oosRatio, 1)} times the out-of-specification exposure whenever the defect penalty is set low.`));
addS(tcap('Table S2. ', 'Operating characteristics of the monitoring charts in batches of 20 items (20,000 simulated run lengths per row). P(≤12): probability of a signal within 12 batches, the longest post-shift window in Study 2.'));
addS(table(['True p', 'ARL CUSUM', 'ARL EWMA', 'P(≤12) CUSUM', 'P(≤12) EWMA'], arlRows, [1500, 1800, 1800, 1950, 1950]));
const t6 = [];
[15, 35, 70].forEach(pen => [2, 6, 18].forEach(rb => { const r = rk('severe_shift', 0, pen, rb), q = rk('severe_shift', 3, pen, rb); t6.push([`${pen} / ${rb}`, f(r.net_WS, 1), f(r.net_FX, 1), f(r.net_AC, 1), f(r.net_AE, 1), r.best, q.best]); }));
addS(tcap('Table S3. ', 'Severe-shift scenario: mean net value by defect penalty and rollback cost under immediate results, and the best policy under immediate results and under a three-period delay. Mean out-of-specification periods with immediate results: ' + POLS.map(p => `${p} ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, p).mean_oos_periods, 2)}`).join(', ') + '.'));
addS(table(['c_d / R', 'WS', 'FX', 'AC', 'AE', 'Best (L=0)', 'Best (L=3)'], t6, [1300, 1150, 1150, 1150, 1150, 1500, 1500]));

add(P(`Delay did, however, change which policies could satisfy a quality constraint. We chose the policy with the highest mean net value subject to a limit on mean out-of-specification periods per run (severe shift, base costs). With a limit of two periods, the immediate-result evaluation selected ${rcf(2, 0).best_constrained} (net value ${f(rcf(2, 0).net_constrained, 1)}, ${f(rcf(2, 0).oos_constrained, 2)} periods) instead of ${rcf(2, 0).best_value_only}; with a report delay of one period or more, no policy met the limit, because each period of delay added about one out-of-specification period for the monitoring policies (AC: ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, 'AC').mean_oos_periods, 2)}, ${f(sm('severe_shift', 1, 'AC').mean_oos_periods, 2)} and ${f(sm('severe_shift', 3, 'AC').mean_oos_periods, 2)} periods at L = 0, 1 and 3). With a limit of four periods, a feasible policy existed up to L = ${feasMax(4)} and none beyond. An immediate-result evaluation would therefore certify a policy as compliant when, with realistic reporting, it is not. Proposition 2 predicted this behaviour from the immediate-feedback runs alone: across the four policies and delays of 1–4 periods, predicted and simulated mean exposure differed by at most ${f(Math.max(...N.exp.map(r => r.abs_error)), 3)} periods, and for AC each period of delay added ${f(ex('AC', 1).slope_pred, 3)} periods, the share of runs in which the shift was detected within the horizon. For exposure limits of 2, 3, 4 and 6 periods the predicted maximum admissible delay for AC was ${['2.0', '3.0', '4.0', '6.0'].map(k => N.lmax.L_max[k].predicted).join(', ')} periods, identical to the simulated values. The run-by-run identity failed where its assumption failed: when the monitoring state at the shift depended on the delay, as for the EWMA chart, it held in only ${pct(Math.min(...N.exp.filter(r => r.policy === 'AE').map(r => r.share_runs_exact)))} of runs at L = 4, and for FX, which almost never rolls back, delay added no exposure at all. A plug-in estimate of L_max is not a certificate: its bounds covered E(L) at all delays simultaneously in only ${pct(Math.min(...N.cert.filter(r => r.policy === 'AC' && r.method === 'plugin').map(r => r.simultaneous_coverage)))}–${pct(Math.max(...N.cert.filter(r => r.policy === 'AC' && r.method === 'plugin').map(r => r.simultaneous_coverage)))} of samples and over-certified the delay in up to ${pct(Math.max(...N.cert.filter(r => r.policy === 'AC' && r.method === 'plugin').map(r => r.false_certification)))} of samples with n = 50 (Supplementary Table S8). The three certified procedures never over-certified and always covered. For AC this is guaranteed when Proposition 2 holds; for the EWMA policy, whose assumption is slightly violated, the full coverage is an empirical finding, not a guarantee. The procedures were conservative: with B = 4 and n = 100, the DKW procedure certified a delay of ${f(ct(4, 100, 'dkw').mean_Lcert, 0)} period in essentially every sample, whereas the Bonferroni Hoeffding and empirical-Bernstein procedures certified mean delays of only ${f(ct(4, 100, 'hoeffding').mean_Lcert, 2)} and ${f(ct(4, 100, 'bernstein').mean_Lcert, 2)} periods; with B = 3 and n = 500 the empirical-Bernstein procedure was the least conservative. No procedure was uniformly best, and several hundred runs were needed before any positive delay could be certified.`));
add(H2('5.7 Failure modes revealed by the delay audit'));
add(P(`Three behaviours would not be visible in an immediate-result evaluation, or would be misjudged in one. First, the WS rule abandons only when no report is pending. With any delay of one period or more, every new pilot creates a pending report, so in the high-defect scenario the rule never abandons and pilots until the horizon. Its value falls from ${f(sm('high_defect', 0, 'WS').mean_net, 2)} under immediate results to ${f(sm('high_defect', 3, 'WS').mean_net, 2)} at L = 3. A corrected rule that stops piloting after five pilots and waits for the pending reports before deciding removes this failure (${f(svv('ws_corrected').highdef_net_WS_L3, 2)} at L = 3; Section 5.8), so it reflects the design of the heuristic rather than delay itself. Second, a chart designed to detect a large shift also reacts to smaller ones. Tuned for p₁ = 0.20, the CUSUM rolled back in ${pct(sm('moderate_shift', 0, 'AC').rollback_rate)} of moderate-shift runs, in which the process remains within specification, and AC was worth ${f(-pd('moderate_shift', 0, 'AC minus WS').mean_diff, 1)} units less than WS there. Its false-rollback rate in the nominal scenario was ${pct(sm('nominal', 0, 'AC').rollback_rate, 1)}. Third, FX never rolled back in the severe-shift scenario (rollback rate ${f(sm('severe_shift', 0, 'FX').rollback_rate, 3)}), because its cumulative rate includes pre-shift pilot and production data and therefore reacts slowly. These are properties of the rule designs; they illustrate why monitoring rules should be designed against the shift that matters for specification and why exposure should be reported with value.`));

add(H2('5.8 Sensitivity analyses'));
const SV = { base: 'Main settings', variable_delay: 'Variable delay (L ± 1 period)', gradual_drift: 'Gradual shift over 6 periods', correlated_defects: 'Correlated defects (ρ = 0.05)', ws_corrected: 'Corrected Wilson-score rule', spec_tuned: 'Charts tuned to the 0.12 specification', long_horizon: '40-period horizon', small_batch: '10 items per batch', strict_plan: 'Acceptance plan c = 2', near_spec_shift: 'Severe shift to p = 0.15' };
const sv = (v) => N.sens.sim.find(r => r.variant === v);
const cal = N.sens.calibration;
const allB = N.sens.sim.flatMap(r => [r.bias3_min, r.bias3_max]);
const ratios = N.sens.sim.map(r => r.severe_oos_FX / r.severe_oos_AC);
add(P(`We repeated Study 2 under nine alternative designs, each with 500 paired seeds (Table S4). Five vary the design parameters, and four address realism: report delays that vary at random by up to one period around L, a gradual shift over six periods instead of a step, correlated defects within a batch (beta-binomial, intra-batch correlation 0.05), and the corrected Wilson-score rule of Section 5.7. In the spec-tuned variant the CUSUM targets p₁ = 0.12 (h = ${f(cal.cusum_h, 2)}, in-control ARL ${f(cal.cusum_arl0, 0)} batches) and the EWMA uses λ = 0.1 (UCL = ${f(cal.ewma_ucl, 3)}, in-control ARL ${f(cal.ewma_arl0, 0)} batches). Across all variants, a three-period delay overstated value by ${f(Math.min(...allB), 1)} to ${f(Math.max(...allB), 1)} units for every policy in the nominal and severe-shift scenarios, and the largest regret from choosing a policy under immediate results was ${f(Math.max(...N.sens.sim.map(r => r.max_regret)), 2)} units. The agreement rate, by contrast, varied from ${pct(Math.min(...N.sens.sim.map(r => r.agreement)))} to ${pct(Math.max(...N.sens.sim.map(r => r.agreement)))}, because near-tied cells flip with sampling noise; this is why we treat regret as the primary summary. Tuning the charts to the specification did not stop rollbacks after the within-specification shift to 0.10 (${pct(sv('spec_tuned').moderate_rollback_AC)} of runs), because a rate of 0.10 cannot be separated quickly from the 0.12 target with batches of 20. In every variant the no-rollback FX rule had a higher net value than AC after a severe shift at the base costs, while spending ${f(Math.min(...ratios), 1)} to ${f(Math.max(...ratios), 1)} times as many periods out of specification.`));
addS(tcap('Table S4. ', 'Study 2 sensitivity analyses (500 paired seeds per variant; base costs). Overstatement: range of V(0) − V(3) over all policies in the nominal and severe-shift scenarios. OOS: mean out-of-specification periods per run after a severe shift, L = 0.'));
addS(table(['Variant', 'Overstatement at L = 3', 'Max regret', 'AC rollback, moderate shift', 'OOS FX / AC', 'Net value FX / AC'],
  Object.keys(SV).map(v => { const r = sv(v); return [SV[v], `${f(r.bias3_min, 1)}–${f(r.bias3_max, 1)}`, f(r.max_regret, 2), pct(r.moderate_rollback_AC), `${f(r.severe_oos_FX, 1)} / ${f(r.severe_oos_AC, 1)}`, `${f(r.severe_net_FX, 1)} / ${f(r.severe_net_AC, 1)}`]; }),
  [2600, 1500, 1000, 1400, 1250, 1250]));
const SC2 = { base: 'Main settings', burn_in_30: 'Burn-in 30%', burn_in_50: 'Burn-in 50%', budget_05: 'Budget 5%', budget_20: 'Budget 20%', window_14d: '14-day refit window' };
const sc = (v, m, L) => N.sens.secom.find(r => r.variant === v && r.model === m && r.delay_days === L);
const t8 = [];
Object.keys(SC2).forEach(v => ['logistic', 'random_forest'].forEach(m => { const a = sc(v, m, 0), b = sc(v, m, 14); t8.push([`${SC2[v]} – ${m === 'logistic' ? 'LR' : 'RF'}`, f(a.random_recall), f(a.retro_recall), `${f(a.replay_recall)} (${f(a.replay_lo)}–${f(a.replay_hi)})`, `${f(b.replay_recall)} (${f(b.replay_lo)}–${f(b.replay_hi)})`, `${a.failures}`]); }));
const ratio14 = Object.keys(SC2).flatMap(v => ['logistic', 'random_forest'].map(m => sc(v, m, 14).replay_recall / sc(v, m, 14).random_recall));
const combos = Object.keys(SC2).flatMap(v => ['logistic', 'random_forest'].map(m => [sc(v, m, 0), sc(v, m, 14)]));
const gapAll = combos.map(([a]) => a.retro_recall - a.replay_recall);
const nRetroAbove = gapAll.filter(g => g > 0).length;
const nLower14 = combos.filter(([a, b]) => b.replay_recall <= a.replay_recall).length;
add(P(`For Study 1 we varied the initial history (30% and 50%), the inspection budget (5% and 20%) and the refit window (14 days), using 20 retrospective splits and 500 bootstrap resamples (Table S5). The retrospective random-split estimate exceeded the immediate-label replay in ${nRetroAbove} of ${combos.length} model–variant combinations (by ${f(Math.min(...gapAll), 2)} to ${f(Math.max(...gapAll), 2)}), so the gap between random-split and time-ordered evaluation is robust. The effect of delay was less consistent: recall at a 14-day delay was at or below the immediate-label value in ${nLower14} of ${combos.length} combinations and ranged from ${f(Math.min(...ratio14), 1)} to ${f(Math.max(...ratio14), 1)} times the random-inspection rate. With 37–46 failures in the replay period, the data support a loss of screening value under delay but cannot pin down its size.`));
addS(tcap('Table S5. ', 'Study 1 sensitivity analyses: recall at budget. Random: expected recall of random inspection. Retrospective: mean over 20 stratified random splits. Replay: deployment replay with 95% day-block bootstrap interval. LR, logistic regression; RF, random forest.'));
addS(table(['Variant – model', 'Random', 'Retrospective', 'Replay, L = 0', 'Replay, L = 14 d', 'Failures in replay'], t8, [2500, 900, 1300, 1700, 1700, 900]));

addS(tcap('Table S6. ', 'Study 3: online release threshold, full grid of feedback delays D and step sizes γ, with the static 1%-slip rule on identical scores. K: confirmed defects; M: largest number of released defects awaiting confirmation; bound: Proposition 1.'));
addS(table(['D – rule', 'Slip', 'VR', 'Weeks > 1%', 'Worst week', 'K', 'M', '|dev|', 'Bound'],
  N.s3o.map(r => [`${r.delay_days} – ${r.method.replace('online, gamma=', 'online, γ = ')}`, p1(r.slip), pct(r.volume_reduction), `${r.weeks_above} / ${r.weeks}`, p1(r.max_weekly_slip), r.K == null ? '—' : r.K, r.M == null ? '—' : r.M, r.dev == null ? '—' : p1(r.dev), r.bound == null ? '—' : p1(r.bound)]),
  [2300, 800, 800, 1000, 1000, 800, 600, 800, 900]));
addS(new Paragraph({ heading: HeadingLevel.HEADING_1, children: [new TextRun('Appendix S1. Proofs')] }));
addS(P(`Proof of Proposition 1. Summing the updates over the K confirmations gives θ_K − θ₀ = γ Σₖ (α − eₖ), so |K⁻¹ Σₖ eₖ − α| ≤ (|θ_K| + |θ₀|)/(γK) and it suffices to bound |θ_K| ≤ b + γ(M + 1). Upward steps (by γα) come only from inspected defects, which are confirmed at once. If θ > b, every new alarm is released because all scores lie in (−b, b), so no new inspected defect occurs and θ cannot increase further; since a single step crosses b by at most γα, θ ≤ b + γα throughout. Downward steps (by γ(1 − α)) come only from confirmations of released defects. If θ < −b, no alarm is released, so the only downward steps until θ returns above −b are confirmations of defects released earlier and still awaiting feedback, at most M of them; together with the step that crossed −b, θ ≥ −b − γ(1 − α)(M + 1). Hence |θ_K| ≤ b + γ(M + 1), which proves the claim. For a fixed delay h, M + 1 can be replaced by h, which recovers the bound of Wang and Hyndman (2024, Corollary 1); for h = 1 it recovers Angelopoulos, Candès and Tibshirani (2023, Proposition 1). The bound was also checked numerically on 200 adversarial score sequences with abrupt and random-walk drift, random delays and arbitrary initial thresholds (largest ratio of deviation to bound 0.78; unit test in the accompanying code).`));
addS(P(`Proof of Proposition 2. Under the assumption, the monitoring statistic computed on post-shift batches takes the same values under every delay; only the period in which each value becomes available moves by L. If the rule would signal after Z post-shift production periods under immediate results, it signals L periods later under delay L, and production continues until then unless the horizon ends first, so exposure is min(H, Z + L) for a deployed run and 0 otherwise. Taking expectations gives E(L). The increment min(H, Z + L + 1) − min(H, Z + L) equals 1 if Z + L < H and 0 otherwise, which gives E(L + 1) − E(L) = P(D = 1, Z + L < H) ≤ 1, so E is non-decreasing in L and L_max(B) is well defined. The assumption fails when the monitoring state at the shift depends on L, for example when an EWMA or CUSUM statistic has accumulated a different number of pre-shift batches because deployment occurred later.`));
addS(tcap('Table S7. ', 'Study 3, 7-day discovery delay: calibration baselines on identical scores (full feedback) and the online threshold under selective feedback with randomized post-release audits (q; five audit randomizations for 0 < q < 1). VR: volume reduction net of audited false calls. Bound: Proposition 3 with δ = 0.05.'));
addS(table(['Method', 'q', 'Slip', 'VR (net)', 'Audited', 'Weeks > 1%', 'Bound'],
  N.sel.map(r => [r.method.replace(' (no other feedback)', '').replace(' (full feedback)', ''), r.q == null ? '—' : r.q, p1(r.slip), pct(r.volume_reduction_net), p1(r.audit_share_of_alarms), `${r.weeks_above} / ${r.weeks}`, r.bound == null ? '—' : p1(r.bound)]),
  [3300, 600, 900, 900, 900, 1000, 900]));
addS(tcap('Table S8. ', 'Certified latency requirement (AC, severe shift, δ = 0.05, 1,000 samples per cell). Coverage: share of samples whose bounds cover E(L) at every delay simultaneously; false: share certifying a delay above the true maximum; mean L: mean certified delay among samples certifying any delay.'));
addS(table(['B', 'n', 'Method', 'Coverage', 'False', 'Mean L', 'None certified'],
  N.cert.filter(r => r.policy === 'AC').map(r => [r.B, r.n, r.method, pct(r.simultaneous_coverage), pct(r.false_certification), r.mean_Lcert == null ? '—' : f(r.mean_Lcert, 2), pct(r.share_none_certified)]),
  [700, 700, 1300, 1100, 1000, 1000, 1300]));
addS(P(`Proof of Proposition 3. The threshold path argument of Proposition 1 applies with downward steps of size γ(1 − α)/q: if θ > b no alarm is inspected, so θ ≤ b + γα; if θ < −b no alarm is released, so only the at most M audited released defects awaiting results can lower θ, giving θ ≥ −b − γ(1 − α)M/q. Summing the updates, |Σᵢ wᵢ(α − eᵢ)| ≤ (b + |θ₀| + γ·max(α, (1 − α)M/q))/γ, where wᵢ = 1 for inspected defects and wᵢ = Oᵢ/q for released ones, Oᵢ indicating an audit. The difference Σᵢ(α − eᵢ) − Σᵢ wᵢ(α − eᵢ) equals Σ over released defects of Xᵢ = (Oᵢ/q − 1)(1 − α). Given the past, the release decision is fixed and Oᵢ is an independent Bernoulli(q) draw, so the Xᵢ are martingale differences with |Xᵢ| ≤ (1 − α)/q and conditional variance at most (1 − α)²(1 − q)/q; their total conditional variance is at most N(1 − α)²(1 − q)/q because there are at most N released defects. Freedman's inequality gives P(|Σ Xᵢ| ≥ x) ≤ 2exp(−x²/(2(V + Rx/3))) with V and R these bounds, and x = (1 − α)[2 log(2/δ)/(3q) + (2N(1 − q)/q · log(2/δ))^{1/2}] satisfies the required inequality because (a² + c²)^{1/2} ≤ a + c. Dividing by N gives the claim. The deterministic term assumes that all audit results have been processed; if P audited released defects are still awaiting results at the horizon, their contributions are missing from the threshold path and the deterministic term increases by at most (1 − α)P/(qN). The martingale argument needs only that each audit is an independent Bernoulli(q) draw given the past and that the release decision is fixed before the draw; it therefore remains valid when scores come from models retrained on audit results already received, but not when scores depend on future audits. In 600 synthetic runs with abrupt drift the bound was never exceeded (largest ratio 0.37).`));
addS(P(`Corollary 1 (finite horizon). Let N_T defects have occurred by time T, of which C_T are confirmed and P_T = N_T − C_T pending, and let R_T and R_T^conf be the slip over all and over confirmed defects (both denominators positive). Then |R_T − R_T^conf| ≤ P_T/N_T, because the two ratios differ only through the P_T pending defects. Under full feedback with discovery delay D, pending defects are released alarms from the last D days, so P_T ≤ P̂_T, the number of alarms released in (T − D, T], which is observable; since x/(C_T + x) increases in x, |R_T − R_T^conf| ≤ P̂_T/(C_T + P̂_T). Combined with Proposition 1 applied to the confirmed defects, this gives an observable bound on |R_T − α|. The inequality is elementary; its usefulness depends on P̂_T being small relative to C_T, which fails when many more alarms are released than there are defects.`));
addS(P(`Corollary 2 (certified latency). With U as in Section 4.6 and empirical distribution F̂ₙ of n independent runs, Massart's form of the Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz inequality gives P(F(u) ≥ F̂ₙ(u) − ε for all u) ≥ 1 − δ for ε = (log(1/δ)/(2n))^{1/2}. On this event F is stochastically dominated by the distribution obtained from F̂ₙ by moving mass ε from its lowest values to a point at or above every possible value; for every nondecreasing g_L bounded by H, E_F[g_L(U)] is therefore at most the mean of g_L under that distribution, with the moved mass evaluated at H. As the event does not depend on L, the bounds hold simultaneously for all L, and the certified delay is correct with probability at least 1 − δ provided Proposition 2 holds.`));
addS(new Paragraph({ heading: HeadingLevel.HEADING_1, children: [new TextRun('Appendix S2. Audited release, additional baselines and certified latency: methods')] }));
addS(P(`In practice a released alarm may never be labelled. We therefore also evaluate a selective-feedback version in which released alarms receive a label only if they are audited after release, independently with probability q, with the result available one day later, while inspected defects are confirmed at once. With inverse-propensity (Horvitz and Thompson 1952) weights, an inspected defect moves the threshold by γα and an audited released defect by γ(α − 1)/q. Proposition 3. Treat the sequence of alarms and labels as fixed and let the scores be predictable, that is, computed only from information available before each decision (models may therefore be retrained on audit results already received), let N be the number of defects over the horizon and M the largest number of audited released defects awaiting results. Then, with probability at least 1 − δ over the audit draws, the realized slip over all N defects satisfies |N⁻¹ Σᵢ eᵢ − α| ≤ (b + |θ₀| + γ·max(α, (1 − α)M/q))/(γN) + (1 − α)[2 log(2/δ)/(3q) + (2N(1 − q)/q · log(2/δ))^{1/2}]/N. The first term is the deterministic threshold-path argument of Proposition 1 with larger downward steps; the second follows from Freedman's (1975) martingale inequality applied to the audit randomization (Appendix S1). Because the audit-driven estimate is unbiased only with q > 0, slip is not identifiable without audits or other feedback: two label sequences that agree on inspected alarms but differ on released ones produce identical observations. The construction combines inverse-propensity weighting with online quantile tracking and is close to adaptive conformal inference with intermittent feedback (Wang, Zecchin and Simeone 2025); we regard it as an applied extension. We evaluate q = 0, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5 and 1 with five audit randomizations each, a 7-day discovery delay for the full-feedback comparators, and three further baselines on identical scores: a sliding-window quantile of confirmed defect scores (28 days), an exponentially weighted quantile (half-life 14 days) and a Clopper–Pearson window rule that chooses the largest threshold whose one-sided 95% upper bound on slip among defects confirmed in the last 28 days is at most 1%. Volume reduction is reported net of audited false calls. At each week end we also compare the actual slip over all defects to date with an observable bound that counts every alarm released within the discovery delay as potentially defective (Corollary 1, Appendix S1).`));
addS(P(`A plug-in estimate of L_max carries no statement of confidence. Under the assumptions of Proposition 2, each run's exposure at delay L is a nondecreasing function, bounded by H, of a single quantity U (−1 for runs that do not deploy, Z otherwise). A one-sided Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz band for the distribution of U (Dvoretzky, Kiefer and Wolfowitz 1956; Massart 1990), with half-width ε = (log(1/δ)/(2n))^{1/2}, therefore yields upper bounds on E(L) that hold simultaneously for all delays with probability at least 1 − δ: move mass ε from the smallest observations to the value H and recompute the mean (Corollary 2, Appendix S1). The certified delay is the largest candidate whose bound is at most B, or none. Because one band serves all delays, no multiplicity correction is needed. We compare it with the plug-in estimate and with Bonferroni-corrected Hoeffding and empirical-Bernstein (Maurer and Pontil 2009) bounds, using 1,000 samples of n = 50 to 1,000 immediate-feedback runs drawn from 20,000 reference runs, with the true E(L) obtained by direct simulation at each delay, δ = 0.05 and limits B = 2, 3 and 4.`));
// 6 Discussion
add(H1('6. Discussion'));
add(H2('6.1 Implications for practice'));
add(P(`The three studies point the same way. On both production datasets, a random-split evaluation suggested much more value than a time-ordered replay delivered. On the AOI line, a release rule designed for 1% slip realized ${p1(s3r(0).slip_thr)} and broke its target in ${s3r(0).weeks_slip_above_target} of ${s3r(0).weeks} weeks, which a random-split evaluation would never have revealed. The size-matched experiments show that the optimism had different sources in the two settings: units from the same period on the AOI line and later production on SECOM. The effect of label delay depended on the process: on SECOM, screening value tended to decline as labels arrived later, although the intervals included zero, and a model that was never retrained was close to random inspection, whereas on the AOI line neither delay nor retraining changed realized slip. The ageing analysis reconciles the two: delay matters when models lose value over the span of the delay, as on SECOM, and not when value is lost in one step at deployment and then stays flat, as on the AOI line. On the AOI line, a confidence-bound threshold reduced slip towards the 1% requirement without guaranteeing it, and only by releasing a small fraction of false calls, a trade-off that a random-split evaluation conceals. In simulation, every decision rule was worth substantially less once report delay was respected, and the source of the shortfall depended on the situation: time to deployment in a stable process, time to detection after a degradation. For a quality engineer preparing a business case, a random-split or immediate-result figure is likely to be optimistic and should not be read as an estimate of deployed value, and a requirement such as a slip limit should be verified on time-ordered data before deployment.`));
add(P(`The simulation also suggests where effort pays off. Where delay mainly postpones deployment, shortening pilot reporting or using a sequential plan that can accept early is most valuable. Where it mainly postpones detection, faster in-line proxies or shorter metrology queues limit exposure. Because rankings were robust to delay but not to the cost ratio, the most consequential input to a policy comparison is the defect penalty, which should be elicited carefully and varied in sensitivity analysis. Ranking by value alone can also hide whether a quality constraint is attainable: with report delay, no simulated policy met an exposure limit that an immediate-result evaluation judged attainable, so constraints should be checked under realistic reporting.`));
add(H2('6.2 A reporting protocol'));
add(P(`Table 8 lists the information we recommend reporting with any evaluation of a quality-prediction model or quality decision rule. Each item addresses a failure observed in these studies and is linked to the data-quality dimension it assesses (Wang and Strong 1996): timeliness (when quality outcomes become usable), completeness (which outcomes are never observed), fitness for purpose (whether the evaluation data and metrics match the decision) and traceability (whether data provenance, decision timing and results can be reproduced). Used in this way, the protocol doubles as a data-quality assessment of the evaluation itself.`));
add(tcap('Table 8. ', 'Reporting protocol for decision evaluations in quality engineering.'));
add(table(['Item', 'Data-quality dimension', 'What to report'], [
  ['1. Label availability', 'Timeliness; completeness', 'When each quality result becomes usable, or an assumed delay with a sensitivity range, and which results are never observed (for example released alarms), with any audit rate used to recover them.'],
  ['2. Time-respecting evaluation', 'Fitness for purpose', 'A deployment replay or prequential evaluation that uses only released labels, alongside any random-split estimate, with size-matched comparisons showing where any gap comes from.'],
  ['3. Decision timing', 'Timeliness; fitness for purpose', 'Whether decisions are made per batch or unit by unit; for unit-by-unit decisions, thresholds fixed from earlier data only.'],
  ['4. Model ageing curve', 'Timeliness', 'Performance as a function of model age over the range of realistic label delays, to judge whether delay matters.'],
  ['5. Budget-based metrics', 'Fitness for purpose', 'Recall and precision at the actual inspection budget, with the random-inspection reference and the number of failures.'],
  ['6. Matched comparators', 'Fitness for purpose', 'Identical actions, information and random streams for all policies, including at least one standard SPC tool with stated ARL.'],
  ['7. Value and exposure', 'Timeliness; fitness for purpose', 'Net value together with defects and out-of-specification periods, policy choice under explicit quality constraints, the maximum admissible reporting delay for an exposure limit (certified, not plug-in), and a mechanism decomposition when delay matters.'],
  ['8. Cost sensitivity and regret', 'Fitness for purpose', 'Results over a grid of defect penalties and rollback costs, the frequency of ranking changes, and the regret.'],
  ['9. Reproducibility', 'Traceability', 'Data provenance, code, seeds, input hashes and a single command that regenerates every reported number.'],
], [2300, 1900, 4800]));
add(H2('6.3 Eliciting delays and costs'));
add(P(`Applying the protocol requires two inputs that Study 2 had to assume. Report delay can be measured from manufacturing execution records as the time between a unit's processing step and the release of its test or metrology result, by product and test type. Costs should be estimated on a common horizon and currency: scrap and rework from material, labour and disposal records; pilot cost from metrology time, consumables and displaced production; downstream defect cost from warranty, returns and customer penalties; and rollback cost from retooling, downtime, restart verification and lost capacity. Each estimate should carry an uncertainty range and be checked for double counting between the defect and rollback terms, and the cost sweep should then be repeated with the elicited values.`));
add(H2('6.4 Limitations'));
add(P(`Study 1 uses a small dataset with ${N.s1.fail_deploy} failures in the replay period, so its delay contrasts are imprecise; the delays in Studies 1 and 3 are imposed scenarios, because neither dataset records label-release times, so the results show potential rather than realized consequences of delay; and SECOM contains no intervention outcomes, so it cannot validate the simulated deployment and rollback decisions. The studies are complementary rather than causally linked. Study 2 assumes, in its main design, independent binomial defects, a single step shift, constant delays, a 20-period horizon and illustrative costs, although sensitivity analyses relax the first three; the chart and plan parameters were reasonable but not optimized, and the heuristic rules are not optimal policies. Different designs could change the specific numbers, and possibly the finding that delay rarely alters rankings. Confirming the results requires plant data with recorded label-release times and, ideally, prospective evaluation. Study 3 is large but covers one line over about 132 days, with anonymized features and timestamps, manual-inspection labels of unknown error rate, and an illustrative slip target; we tested three model classes and four threshold procedures but did not search exhaustively, and drift-adaptive methods might narrow the gap. The online threshold assumes that released defects are eventually discovered or audited; the audit experiment simulates the audit mechanism on retrospective labels, which were available for all alarms in the dataset, and its guarantees are long-run or wide at the horizons studied. The latency certificates rely on Proposition 2 and on independent runs. Propositions 1 and 2 are adaptations of known arguments, offered as practical tools rather than as new theory. The ageing curves are pooled over a few anchors and, for SECOM, have wide intervals, and the size-matched comparison, repeated with three independent half-week and training samples, gave stable directions but varying significance; its calibration sets differ between designs.`));

// 7 Conclusions
add(H1('7. Conclusions'));
add(P(`Evaluating quality decisions with information that would not have been available at decision time made them look better than they performed. On two production datasets, time-ordered replays delivered much less than random-split evaluation promised: SECOM screening fell well short of its random-split estimate, and on a Siemens inspection line a release rule designed for 1% slip realized ${p1(s3r(0).slip_thr)} and exceeded its target in ${s3r(0).weeks_slip_above_target} of ${s3r(0).weeks} weeks. Size-matched experiments traced the optimism to units from the same period on the inspection line and to later production on SECOM, and strictly causal decisions gave the same conclusions. Neither other models nor a more conservative threshold closed the gap without giving up most of the benefit. Longer label delay was associated with lower screening value on SECOM, although the intervals included zero, and not with lower value on the inspection line, consistent with how quickly models aged in each setting. In simulation, report delay removed substantial value through slower deployment or slower detection and rarely changed the value ranking of policies, but it made an exposure limit unattainable that an immediate-result evaluation judged attainable. Value should therefore be reported together with out-of-specification exposure and checked against quality constraints under realistic reporting. The reporting protocol in Table 8 and the accompanying code are intended to make these checks routine.`));

